# Task 5 report — persist complete optimizer V5 learning

## Outcome

Implemented the pure durable-memory domain and the contained local artifact repository within the
three-file Task 5 scope. No provider, Docker, replay, market-data, Git mutation outside the final
focused commit, or test-file change was performed.

Source scope:

- Created `core/pit_optimizer_v5/memory.py`.
- Created `core/pit_optimizer_v5/artifacts.py`.
- Modified the infrastructure-free dependency leaf `core/pit_optimizer_v5/contracts.py` only to
  expose its existing canonical primitive/JSON/digest convention, add canonical artifact-ref
  serialization, and expose stable hypothesis/critic digests.

## Domain and identity contracts

- `ExperimentRecordV5` uses the real identity union:
  `ExperimentIdentityV5 | PreValidationInvalidExperimentIdentityV5`. Only `status="invalid"` with
  failed validation accepts the pre-validation identity and null policy revision. Every other
  status requires a valid post-validation identity and exact non-null revision.
- Record construction re-derives and compares the parent, full hypothesis, structural template,
  strict assignment, round, discovery-plan digest, and optional policy-revision bindings. The
  duplicated template digest, parent, changed-symbol set, evidence policy identities, campaign
  episode tuple, campaign discovery-plan digest, experiment ID, and critic experiment ID must all
  agree.
- Status/evidence combinations are closed. Exact duplicates must use the parent policy identity;
  suite-equivalent candidates must carry the exact parent semantic fingerprint; a status claiming
  behavioral distinction rejects that parent fingerprint. Quick rejection, complete zero-trade
  campaign, complete evaluated campaign, and partial timeout/cancellation/failure outcomes each
  admit only their intended evidence shape.
- Artifact serialization preserves strict variant literal types with explicit bool/int/float/str
  tags. This avoids conflating a numeric axis value with text while leaving all existing valid V4
  and candidate/experiment identity digests unchanged.
- The complete per-experiment `CriticReviewV5` remains inline in the immutable record. A separate
  `critic_artifact_ref` names the content-addressed full batch artifact exactly once. Repository
  append and load require that artifact to contain exactly one byte-equivalent review for the
  record's experiment, preserving the batch comparative assessment, next-campaign direction, and
  top-level evidence IDs without duplicating the full batch.

## Event journal, recovery, and archive boundary

- The closed event payload union covers exactly the brief's six kinds: round intent, rendered
  variant, quick evidence, one episode evidence item, owned resource lease, and cleanup result.
  Event kind is bound both to its canonical payload path and its decoded concrete payload class.
- `RoundEventV5` binds campaign, round, contiguous sequence, predecessor event digest, kind,
  optional experiment, and payload reference. Recovery authenticates the complete contiguous
  stream, canonicalizes it independently of execution consumers, and returns only declared local
  steps not already present. Resource recovery keys include both resource kind and lease ID, so
  several owned workspaces/workers/evaluators/containers cannot collapse to one completed step.
- Final experiment records are the immutable rebuildable experiment journal; the event-kind set was
  not expanded to create a competing finalization journal. The checkpoint's authenticated,
  canonically ordered, append-only set of final record references is the promotion/commit
  authority. Orphan critic or record files written before checkpoint publication remain durable but
  unpromoted.
- `ArchiveReducerV5` is a typed Task-6-owned reducer/projection boundary. Task 5 defines no archive
  scoring or admission policy. Archive reconstruction folds only checkpoint-authorized records in
  canonical round/experiment order.
- Archive bytes are durably replaced before checkpoint bytes. Checkpoint generation advances
  exactly once, cannot forget finalized records, and permits an exact idempotent replay. Recovery
  treats the checkpoint as authority: an archive-ahead crash is repaired from its prior authorized
  record set, while a checkpoint durably replaced before a caller-side failure is recovered as
  committed.

## Local artifact repository

- Every reference is root-relative canonical POSIX text under the authenticated repository root.
  The concrete grammar additionally rejects colons in every component, Windows reserved names,
  and trailing dot/space forms.
- Reads hold the existing hardened component-relative directory capabilities. POSIX uses
  no-follow directory descriptors; Windows holds non-reparse directory handles without delete
  sharing. Final targets must be single-link regular files, remain the same filesystem object
  across the read, and stay within a fixed byte bound.
- Inputs, typed payloads, events, records, and critic objects are create-only and fsynced. Only
  `archive.json` and `checkpoint.json` use same-directory fsynced temporary files plus atomic
  replace. The existing V4 store behavior was not modified.
- Bytes are authenticated before parsing, JSON rejects duplicate keys and noncanonical encodings,
  and reconstruction re-runs every domain invariant. Recursive graph verification performs
  deterministic DFS and returns the existing typed missing/relocated/cycle/digest-mismatch result.

## Investigator projection

- Durable records are never truncated or overwritten.
- The bounded projection first includes complete selected-parent lineage feedback (full hypothesis,
  validation, measured evidence digests/target gap, every critic-review field, and authenticated
  refs), then as much complete relevant-mechanism feedback as fits, then digest-backed summaries for
  other experiments.
- If the mandatory selected-parent lineage itself cannot fit, projection fails with
  `ProjectionBudgetTooSmallV5`; it never silently truncates complete feedback. Ordering and bytes
  are stable under reversed input order.

